module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Crystalline.CompletedDividedPowerForms

/-!
# The completed divided power ring is `p`-adically complete for its own ideal

`Crystalline.completedDividedPowerRing p (B := B)` is the `p`-adic completion
`D = lim B/(p)ⁿ B` of `B`.  Item (07JB) of [Stacks, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG] asks of a module
`M` over `D` that it be `p`-adically complete, and the ideal in that condition is
`DividedPowers.pAdicIdeal (A := D) p = (p) ⊆ D` — the ideal generated by `p` **in the
completion**, not the extension `((p) ⊆ B).map (algebraMap B D)` of the ideal the completion was
taken along.  Mathlib's `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self` gives the second ideal, so the first
is not a goal `infer_instance` can close: that is what blocked instantiating the column-zero edge
`Crystalline.CosimplicialCompletedModule.columnZeroIso` of the double complex `K` of
[Stacks, Proposition 60.21.3, Tag 07LG] at the structure sheaf, whose standing hypothesis is
`[∀ b, IsAdicComplete (pAdicIdeal (A := E.completedRing p b) p) (W.carrier b)]`.

This module closes that gap, and the reason it is cheap is that the two ideals are **equal**:
`Crystalline.pAdicIdeal_map_ringHom` already says that `(p)` maps onto `(p)` along *any* ring
map, both being generated by the image of the integer `p`.  So the condition over `D` is the
condition Mathlib proves, up to a rewrite of the ideal, and the finite generation
`AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self` asks of `(p) ⊆ B` is free: a principal ideal is finitely
generated.

Nothing here is specific to divided powers — the content is about `AdicCompletion` along a
principal ideal `(p)` — and it is phrased at `Crystalline.completedDividedPowerRing` because that
is the spelling the crystalline files use and the spelling the instance has to match.

## Main results

* `Crystalline.instIsAdicCompleteCompletedDividedPowerRing` — **the completion `D` is
  `p`-adically complete for its own ideal `(p) ⊆ D`**, as an instance.  This is item (07JB) of
  [Stacks, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG] for the module `M = D`, the case of the coefficient sheaf
  `𝒪_{X/S}`.
* `Crystalline.isPAdicallyComplete_completedDividedPowerRing` — the same statement spelled with
  `Crystalline.IsPAdicallyComplete`, the library's own name for item (07JB).

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07JG)
* [Stacks Project, Proposition 60.21.3, Tag 07LG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07LG)
-/

@[expose] public section

open DividedPowers

universe v

namespace Crystalline

variable (p : ℕ) {B : Type v} [CommRing B]

/-- **The `p`-adic completion `D` of `B` is `p`-adically complete for its own ideal `(p) ⊆ D`.**

This is item (07JB) of [Stacks, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG] for the module `M = D` over itself, the
case of the coefficient sheaf `𝒪_{X/S}`.  Mathlib's `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self` proves
it for the extension along `algebraMap B D` of the ideal `(p) ⊆ B`, and
`Crystalline.pAdicIdeal_map_ringHom` identifies that extension with the ideal `(p) ⊆ D`; the
finite generation it asks of `(p) ⊆ B` is `Submodule.fg_span_singleton`. -/
@[stacks 07JB "item (07JB) for the module `M = D` over the completed divided power ring itself"]
public instance instIsAdicCompleteCompletedDividedPowerRing :
    IsAdicComplete (pAdicIdeal (A := completedDividedPowerRing p (B := B)) p)
      (completedDividedPowerRing p (B := B)) :=
  pAdicIdeal_map_ringHom p (algebraMap B (completedDividedPowerRing p (B := B))) ▸
    AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self (pAdicIdeal (A := B) p) (Submodule.fg_span_singleton _)

/-- **Item (07JB) of [Stacks, Lemma 60.17.3, Tag 07JG] for `M = D`**, spelled with the library's
own name for that item. -/
public theorem isPAdicallyComplete_completedDividedPowerRing :
    IsPAdicallyComplete p (completedDividedPowerRing p (B := B))
      (completedDividedPowerRing p (B := B)) :=
  instIsAdicCompleteCompletedDividedPowerRing p

end Crystalline

end
